{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-109094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109094" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3bf1f3eb4ac0b58c45f495103b3782e93754e4b763188a1dc48a6f0aa4c687d"} {"question" "q-109094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109094" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(-1)(2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c3bf1f3eb4ac0b58c45f495103b3782e93754e4b763188a1dc48a6f0aa4c687d"} {"question" "q-109314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca512399446c296316eb39bf7c20f9c6cb5e2b865c73fd611"} {"question" "q-109314" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14074386/state.json" "file_sha256" "c1be3bfb302c288ca5123